The property is **`tokenizerId`** on the outcome returned by the simulator.

In that test (`packages/core/test/cache.test.ts`, in the Zone A describe block — "records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)"), the setup is:

```ts
const heuristic = new ProviderCacheSimulator({ tokenizer: h.tokenizer }).submit(prompt);
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
}).submit(prompt);

expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
expect(bytes.total).not.toBe(heuristic.total);
expect(bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)).toEqual(heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds));
```

So it's `bytes.tokenizerId` — the id stamped on every submitted outcome by the injected `ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', ...)`. The test's point is threefold: the outcome records which tokenizer produced it (`tokenizerId === 'bytes-v0'`), the byte-exact tokenizer yields a different total than the heuristic one (`total` differs, since one counts characters/bytes and the other its own units), but the segmentation itself — which blocks group into which cached segments (`segments[].blockIds`) — is identical between the two, because segmentation depends on breakpoint placement, not token counting.

