The Bash command that reads `all-runs.json` and pulls per-turn inputs for `iter5` and `iter6` (event seq 264, in the diagnosis (2) phase) was given the description:

**"Extract per-turn input distributions"**

For context, that command was:

```
node -e "
const fs = require('fs');
const all = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('.../scratchpad/all-runs.json','utf8'));
const inputs = (label, arm) => all.perRun.filter(r => r.label===label && r.arm===arm).flatMap(r => r.turns.map(t => t.usage.input));
console.log('CT5='+JSON.stringify(inputs('iter5','context-tree')));
console.log('CT6='+JSON.stringify(inputs('iter6','context-tree')));
"
```

Its output (CT5 marching up to ~15k tokens/turn vs CT6 mostly staying under ~4k with phase-boundary resets) is what backed the conclusion recorded in that branch: input token growth follows an O(delta) pattern in optimized versions rather than O(trace), validating the context-tree summarization approach.
